00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:31,000
Scout MasterChip in seven minutes or less?

47
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:42,000
In 1920, Baden Powell wrote a booklet called

48
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:43,000
Aged Scout MasterChip

49
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:46,000
And within that booklet he lays out these five

50
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:50,000
fundamental things for applying the patrol method.

51
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:54,000
They're just as true now as they were the day that he wrote them,

52
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:56,000
So let's take a look at them.

53
00:03:56,000 --> 00:03:58,000
The first is the formation of patrols.

54
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:00,000
Now Baden Powell said six to eight,

55
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:02,000
and that's still a pretty good rule of thumb.

56
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:05,000
We scouts, although, have fallen into the habit

57
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:09,000
of assigning scouts to patrols according to our idea

58
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:12,000
of what best serves our concerns,

59
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:16,000
Almost to the exclusion of the scouts' own choice.

60
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:19,000
Scouts ought to choose their patrol,

61
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:21,000
And we're going to talk a little bit more about that

62
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:25,000
in one of the email answers that's coming up in this podcast.

63
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:29,000
The second fundamental is real free-handed responsibility,

64
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:31,000
And that's exactly the way Baden Powell put it:

65
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:33,000
Free-handed responsibility.

66
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:36,000
Patrol leaders have to have actual responsibility.

67
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:39,000
They have to be allowed to think and plan for their patrol.

68
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:41,000
This is a delicate business.

69
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:45,000
Too much training and mentoring and oversight from adults

70
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:48,000
is going to smother the flame of initiative

71
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:51,000
that we want to see in our patrol leaders.

72
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:53,000
Feed that fire slowly.

73
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:58,000
Partial responsibility only brings partial results.

74
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:02,000
Number three is that patrols are independent, distinct

75
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:04,000
and autonomous.

76
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:06,000
They have to stand on their own.

77
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:07,000
They need their own identity.

78
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:10,000
That's why we have a patrol name and a patrol flag,

79
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:12,000
a patrol yell and all that kind of great stuff.

80
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:16,000
Now this extends to every aspect of the program.

81
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:18,000
When they're camping together,

82
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:21,000
each patrol has their own area, their own food,

83
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:23,000
their own leadership and you know,

84
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:25,000
they can follow their own program

85
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:27,000
Elsewhere. in his writings Baden Powell,

86
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:29,000
when asked if patrols could go camping together,

87
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:32,000
said that he was a little skeptical about the idea

88
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:34,000
because it would tend to make scouts

89
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:36,000
into one big, giant group to be administered.

90
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:39,000
He said that if a couple of patrols went camping together

91
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:43,000
well, he wouldn't necessarily say that it was a bad idea,

92
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:47,000
but he'd want them to be at least a quarter mile apart.

93
00:05:47,000 --> 00:05:50,000
In practical terms we're not going to be able to do that

94
00:05:50,000 --> 00:05:52,000
in most of the places that we go camping.

95
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:56,000
But giving patrols their own distinct area,

96
00:05:56,000 --> 00:05:58,000
letting them decide what they're going to eat

97
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:01,000
and go out and buy the food and prepare it for themselves,

98
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:05,000
and exercising their own leadership, their own oversight

99
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:09,000
and even their own program during campouts, is a great idea.

100
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:12,000
The fourth fundamental of the patrol method

101
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:17,000
is emulation and competition between patrols.

102
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:21,000
Spirited good nature competition amongst patrols

103
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:24,000
helps that patrol spirit grow and flourish.

104
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:28,000
Games and contests among patrols build individual

105
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:32,000
as well as group character, as scouts realize

106
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:35,000
that their individual contribution means something

107
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:38,000
that it adds to his patrol's honor and spirit.

108
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:41,000
And the fifth fundamental of the patrol method

109
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:43,000
is the Patrol Leaders Council.

110
00:06:43,000 --> 00:06:46,000
The tone of everything the patrol does

111
00:06:46,000 --> 00:06:49,000
is set by the Patrol Leaders Council,

112
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:53,000
exercising real authority and decision-making power

113
00:06:53,000 --> 00:06:55,000
in choosing and preparing for activities.

114
00:06:55,000 --> 00:06:58,000
Now, naturally, they're making all those decisions

115
00:06:58,000 --> 00:07:01,000
and making all those plans in the context

116
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:03,000
of the goals of scouting.

117
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:06,000
We work with them to understand what those goals

118
00:07:06,000 --> 00:07:09,000
and what that context is and then we get them moving.

119
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:11,000
Is it really that simple?

120
00:07:11,000 --> 00:07:13,000
If you just follow those five fundamentals,

121
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:15,000
do you have the patrol method?

122
00:07:15,000 --> 00:07:17,000
Yeah, it's really that simple.

123
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:19,000
but a lot of things in life are simple

124
00:07:19,000 --> 00:07:22,000
but they take a lot of study and application

125
00:07:22,000 --> 00:07:24,000
and practice to master right

126
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:26,000
Swinging a hammer or a golf club.

127
00:07:26,000 --> 00:07:28,000
I can show you how to do that

128
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:30,000
and you can do that in 20 seconds.

129
00:07:30,000 --> 00:07:32,000
That's going to take a little while.

130
00:07:32,000 --> 00:07:34,000
If we begin with those five fundamentals

131
00:07:34,000 --> 00:07:36,000
and we stick to them,

132
00:07:36,000 --> 00:07:38,000
we're going to see the patrol method

133
00:07:38,000 --> 00:07:40,000
do exactly what it was intended to do

134
00:07:40,000 --> 00:07:43,000
and that is to achieve the aims of scouting.

135
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:04,000
Write me a letter,

136
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:06,000
Send it by name.

137
00:08:08,000 --> 00:08:10,000
Email. that is folks.

138
00:08:10,000 --> 00:08:13,000
And here's an answer to one of your emails.

139
00:08:22,000 --> 00:08:24,000
So I got this email from Robert

140
00:08:24,000 --> 00:08:26,000
and Robert said: first, let me say thank you

141
00:08:26,000 --> 00:08:28,000
for the emails that I've subscribed to,

142
00:08:28,000 --> 00:08:31,000
Love reading them for ideas and suggestions.

143
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:33,000
I'm a scout master and I've taken over

144
00:08:33,000 --> 00:08:35,000
just a few months ago

145
00:08:35,000 --> 00:08:37,000
I'm a bit younger, I'm 30 years old.

146
00:08:37,000 --> 00:08:39,000
I don't have a child in the troop,

147
00:08:39,000 --> 00:08:41,000
but my nephew is a member.

148
00:08:41,000 --> 00:08:43,000
I'm an Eagle Scout and I was asked by the committee

149
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:45,000
to step in and be the scout master

150
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:47,000
and help out with our troop.

151
00:08:47,000 --> 00:08:50,000
We have about 20 active scouts.

152
00:08:50,000 --> 00:08:52,000
Prior to my becoming scout master,

153
00:08:52,000 --> 00:08:55,000
siblings were permitted on campouts

154
00:08:55,000 --> 00:08:57,000
and I really don't approve of it-

155
00:08:57,000 --> 00:09:00,000
Both brothers and sisters, older and younger.

156
00:09:00,000 --> 00:09:02,000
then the scouts come along

157
00:09:02,000 --> 00:09:04,000
and they really become a distraction

158
00:09:04,000 --> 00:09:06,000
for the scouts and the adult leaders,

159
00:09:06,000 --> 00:09:09,000
as well as all the other issues and concerns

160
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:11,000
you may imagine arise.

161
00:09:11,000 --> 00:09:13,000
When I talk about this, I'm getting a lot of pushback.

162
00:09:13,000 --> 00:09:15,000
People involved with the troop right now

163
00:09:15,000 --> 00:09:17,000
argue that scouting is a family activity.

164
00:09:17,000 --> 00:09:19,000
I'd like to hear your thoughts on this.

165
00:09:19,000 --> 00:09:21,000
Well, Robert, first of all,

166
00:09:21,000 --> 00:09:23,000
congratulations for taking on the scout master job.

167
00:09:23,000 --> 00:09:25,000
Of course, the next step,

168
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:27,000
whether you're an Eagle Scout or not,

169
00:09:27,000 --> 00:09:29,000
is getting yourself trained.

170
00:09:29,000 --> 00:09:31,000
Go to training and that way

171
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:33,000
you're going to have the answers

172
00:09:33,000 --> 00:09:35,000
to these questions before they're asked.

173
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:37,000
When a scout troop goes camping,

174
00:09:37,000 --> 00:09:39,000
it's not family camping.

175
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:41,000
I'm aware that some troops conduct a special camp out

176
00:09:41,000 --> 00:09:43,000
where family members are invited

177
00:09:43,000 --> 00:09:45,000
once a year or so.

178
00:09:45,000 --> 00:09:47,000
but scout camping is about

179
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:49,000
scouts camping with their patrol

180
00:09:49,000 --> 00:09:51,000
Full stop.

181
00:09:51,000 --> 00:09:53,000
What you describe as not scouting.

182
00:09:53,000 --> 00:09:55,000
It's not even remotely like no little brothers and sisters

183
00:09:55,000 --> 00:09:57,000
or unregistered big brothers and sisters

184
00:09:57,000 --> 00:09:59,000
or unregistered parents,

185
00:09:59,000 --> 00:10:01,000
unless they're required to meet

186
00:10:01,000 --> 00:10:03,000
youth protection guidelines,

187
00:10:03,000 --> 00:10:05,000
Then you only need one

188
00:10:05,000 --> 00:10:07,000
Scouts work with and share tents with

189
00:10:07,000 --> 00:10:09,000
the other scouts in their patrol,

190
00:10:09,000 --> 00:10:11,000
Not anybody else.

191
00:10:11,000 --> 00:10:13,000
If we aren't doing this,

192
00:10:13,000 --> 00:10:15,000
we aren't really doing scouting.

193
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:17,000
You were chosen to serve as a scout master,

194
00:10:17,000 --> 00:10:19,000
so it's up to you to be a scout master,

195
00:10:19,000 --> 00:10:21,000
not a family camping coordinator.

196
00:10:21,000 --> 00:10:23,000
When you're in your shoes,

197
00:10:23,000 --> 00:10:25,000
I would tell- not ask,

198
00:10:25,000 --> 00:10:27,000
but tell- the troop committee

199
00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:29,000
that you're going to take only scouts

200
00:10:29,000 --> 00:10:31,000
and registered adult leaders

201
00:10:31,000 --> 00:10:33,000
on the next camping trip

202
00:10:33,000 --> 00:10:35,000
because you'd like to actually do scouting.

203
00:10:35,000 --> 00:10:37,000
Explain that there is simply no provision

204
00:10:37,000 --> 00:10:39,000
anywhere in your training

205
00:10:39,000 --> 00:10:41,000
for taking anyone other than that.

206
00:10:41,000 --> 00:10:43,000
Now they're going to push back

207
00:10:43,000 --> 00:10:45,000
and when they push back,

208
00:10:45,000 --> 00:10:47,000
explain that they've asked you to be a scout master.

209
00:10:47,000 --> 00:10:49,000
in order for you to be the scout master,

210
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:51,000
You're going to be able to take a scout master

211
00:10:51,000 --> 00:10:53,000
program according to your knowledge and training

212
00:10:53,000 --> 00:10:55,000
and you're going to expect their support

213
00:10:55,000 --> 00:10:57,000
when you do so.

214
00:10:57,000 --> 00:10:59,000
Bring along a scout master handbook

215
00:10:59,000 --> 00:11:01,000
and the scout handbook and ask the committee

216
00:11:01,000 --> 00:11:03,000
to find where it says that scouting

217
00:11:03,000 --> 00:11:05,000
is a family camping program.

218
00:11:05,000 --> 00:11:07,000
They're not going to be able to find it

219
00:11:07,000 --> 00:11:09,000
because it's not there.

220
00:11:09,000 --> 00:11:11,000
Honestly, Robert, if they won't support you in this,

221
00:11:11,000 --> 00:11:13,000
tell them it's time to find another scout master.

222
00:11:13,000 --> 00:11:15,000
If things are this far off the rails

223
00:11:15,000 --> 00:11:17,000
and the committee is unwilling to change

224
00:11:17,000 --> 00:11:19,000
what we're being able to accomplish

225
00:11:19,000 --> 00:11:21,000
much of what a scout master is charged with doing-

226
00:11:21,000 --> 00:11:23,000
I also heard from Derek

227
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:25,000
and he says: I'm a scout master

228
00:11:25,000 --> 00:11:27,000
with less than a year under my belt

229
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:29,000
and I'm faced with a good problem.

230
00:11:29,000 --> 00:11:31,000
We're growing. We currently have four patrols

231
00:11:31,000 --> 00:11:33,000
and we anticipate that we'll get

232
00:11:33,000 --> 00:11:35,000
another seven scouts

233
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:37,000
when we blows crossover in February.

234
00:11:37,000 --> 00:11:39,000
That would mean a fifth patrol.

235
00:11:39,000 --> 00:11:41,000
Currently we have one first year patrol

236
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:43,000
and two second year patrols

237
00:11:43,000 --> 00:11:45,000
and one patrol made up of the remaining older boys.

238
00:11:45,000 --> 00:11:47,000
The third patrol is usually

239
00:11:47,000 --> 00:11:49,000
woefully understaffed during outings

240
00:11:49,000 --> 00:11:51,000
as those scouts are either committed to other obligations

241
00:11:51,000 --> 00:11:53,000
or working. I think the patrols

242
00:11:53,000 --> 00:11:55,000
need to be reworked a bit,

243
00:11:55,000 --> 00:11:57,000
but I don't want to break up any close friends

244
00:11:57,000 --> 00:11:59,000
or turn patrols into popularity contests.

245
00:11:59,000 --> 00:12:01,000
What I'm considering is splitting up the oldest patrol

246
00:12:01,000 --> 00:12:03,000
and putting those scouts into the other three patrols.

247
00:12:03,000 --> 00:12:05,000
Based on your experience,

248
00:12:05,000 --> 00:12:07,000
is this a good idea or a bad idea,

249
00:12:07,000 --> 00:12:09,000
or maybe there's a better way?

250
00:12:09,000 --> 00:12:11,000
I must admit I haven't asked the scouts yet,

251
00:12:11,000 --> 00:12:13,000
but I would anticipate that the older boys

252
00:12:13,000 --> 00:12:15,000
would not really want to be split up.

253
00:12:15,000 --> 00:12:17,000
I should add that there is a two year age difference

254
00:12:17,000 --> 00:12:19,000
between the older boy patrol

255
00:12:19,000 --> 00:12:21,000
and the next oldest patrol.

256
00:12:21,000 --> 00:12:23,000
Plus, I really like the idea of keeping scouts together

257
00:12:23,000 --> 00:12:25,000
in their own patrol,

258
00:12:25,000 --> 00:12:27,000
for the first year at least.

259
00:12:27,000 --> 00:12:29,000
Any suggestions?

260
00:12:29,000 --> 00:12:31,000
Well, Derek, I want to answer your question

261
00:12:31,000 --> 00:12:33,000
by asking a few other questions.

262
00:12:33,000 --> 00:12:35,000
We have to think about these things.

263
00:12:35,000 --> 00:12:37,000
Why are boys in scouting?

264
00:12:37,000 --> 00:12:39,000
Why do we have patrols?

265
00:12:39,000 --> 00:12:41,000
Are patrols just so that we have an efficient system

266
00:12:41,000 --> 00:12:43,000
