271
00:12:51,000 --> 00:12:53,000
Boys are scouts because their friends are involved.

272
00:12:53,000 --> 00:12:55,000
If they don't get to work with their friends,

273
00:12:55,000 --> 00:12:57,000
they're not very interested in scouts.

274
00:12:57,000 --> 00:12:59,000
We have patrols

275
00:12:59,000 --> 00:13:01,000
because basically, there's no scouting

276
00:13:01,000 --> 00:13:03,000
without them.

277
00:13:03,000 --> 00:13:05,000
99% of the aims of scouting

278
00:13:05,000 --> 00:13:07,000
are met in the context of the patrol.

279
00:13:07,000 --> 00:13:09,000
Scouts working together

280
00:13:09,000 --> 00:13:11,000
encounter all kinds of challenges

281
00:13:11,000 --> 00:13:13,000
and they learn all kinds of things.

282
00:13:13,000 --> 00:13:15,000
When they go camping, they go by patrol.

283
00:13:15,000 --> 00:13:17,000
At meetings, they're preparing to go camping

284
00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:19,000
by patrol. Everything in scouting

285
00:13:19,000 --> 00:13:21,000
is about the patrol.

286
00:13:21,000 --> 00:13:23,000
Once you understand this,

287
00:13:23,000 --> 00:13:25,000
you'll agree that scouts ought to be able

288
00:13:25,000 --> 00:13:27,000
to freely choose their patrol.

289
00:13:27,000 --> 00:13:29,000
They know who they get along with

290
00:13:29,000 --> 00:13:31,000
and who their friends are.

291
00:13:31,000 --> 00:13:33,000
To understand how your scouts are going to view

292
00:13:33,000 --> 00:13:35,000
you stepping in to make changes

293
00:13:35,000 --> 00:13:37,000
to their patrols, imagine

294
00:13:37,000 --> 00:13:39,000
the opportunity to evaluate your troop

